The  command allows state information created for NAT entries and
rules using to be locked (modification prevented) and then  saved
to disk, allowing for the system to experience a reboot, followed
by  the restoration of that information, resulting in connections
not being interrupted.  The information are read or stored  using
binary  format.   The  default format is a readable ASCII format,
similar to syntax.  Lock state tables in the kernel.  Restore in‐
formation from and load it into the kernel.  The state tables are
locked at the beginning of this operation and unlocked once  com‐
plete.   Read  information  in  from and load it into the kernel.
This requires the state tables to have already  been  locked  and
does  not  change the lock once complete.  Unlock state tables in
the kernel.  Provide a verbose description of what’s being  done.
Write  information  from  the  kernel out to The state tables are
locked at the beginning of this operation and unlocked once  com‐
plete.   Write  information  from the kernel out to This requires
the state tables to have already been locked and does not  change
the lock once complete.
